Subject: [PATCH] feat(batch2/task3): activate the accepted EMAIL provider
 contract via SMTP

Batch 2 Task 3 -- Notification Provider Activation + First Real
Reservation Email Flow.

Repository analysis found the entire notification domain already
built and thoroughly tested, with exactly one precise gap:

Already accepted and unchanged by this task:
- notification-provider-activation.contracts.ts (G3.29): the full
  deny-by-default activation contract -- EMAIL as the only supported
  channel, exact provider-key/channel matching, 250ms-10s timeout
  bounds, credential-reference-only secret handling (never a raw
  value in the safe config view), ContractNotificationProviderRegistry
  with forChannel()/health(). Already comprehensively tested
  (notification-provider-activation.contracts.spec.ts).
- The full pipeline: outbox-driven consumption
  (ReservationNotificationConsumerService, scoped specifically to the
  already-accepted "reservation ready" lifecycle event -- not
  "created/cancelled/expired", which are not wired end-to-end in this
  repository and were correctly left alone rather than invented),
  live-Postgres tenant-scoped recipient resolution
  (ReservationRecipientResolverService, fail-closed for disabled
  memberships/tenants), deliberately minimal message composition
  (ReservationNotificationComposerService -- static subject/body, zero
  patient or reservation detail), and the delivery worker
  (NotificationWorkerService: bounded retry, dead-letter, idempotent,
  privacy-safe observation records).
- The precise gap: notification.module.ts hardcoded
  NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER_REGISTRY to UnconfiguredNotificationProviderRegistry
  unconditionally -- it never read environment configuration, and no
  concrete adapter implementing ActivatedNotificationProviderAdapter
  existed anywhere in the repository. The contract layer was fully
  built but never actually activated.

Selected provider/adapter: SMTP via nodemailer. Protocol-based, not a
proprietary vendor SDK -- works against any SMTP-compatible provider
(a corporate relay or any vendor's SMTP endpoint) without vendor
fields ever touching the domain layer, satisfying "smallest adapter
that can satisfy the existing provider-neutral contract without
contaminating the domain layer."

New: smtp-notification-provider.adapter.ts
- providerKey 'smtp', channel 'EMAIL'. A single deliver() method
  matching the accepted contract exactly.
- Normalizes real nodemailer/SMTP errors into the accepted
  TRANSIENT/TERMINAL classes (EAUTH and 5xx SMTP responses ->
  TERMINAL; everything else, including connection/timeout failures,
  ->  TRANSIENT, left to the worker's existing bounded retry).
- Applies the activation's timeoutMs to connection/greeting/socket
  timeouts.
- Never logs or persists the connection URL/credential, the
  destination address, the composed subject/body, or the raw
  provider error -- only a normalized code, provider key, and
  classification are ever surfaced.
- Accepts an optional pre-built transport purely as a test seam (real
  nodemailer jsonTransport for zero-network success-path testing);
  production always builds a real transport from the injected
  connection URL.

New: notification-provider-registry.factory.ts
- createNotificationProviderRegistry(environment) reads real
  activation config via the existing parseProviderActivationEnvironment
  contract function. No activation config -> disabled by construction
  (unchanged accepted default). Enabled with an unsupported provider
  key -> falls through to a registry with no adapter, which already
  fails every request closed via the accepted forChannel()/health()
  logic -- no new fail-closed code was needed for that case, only
  correct wiring. Enabled with providerKey 'smtp' -> fetches the
  referenced secret (connection URL) and the from-address via
  @medsphere/config's existing loadEnv, constructing the real adapter.
  A malformed/incomplete activation throws here, failing application
  bootstrap loudly -- the same fail-fast philosophy loadEnv already
  uses for missing required variables, not a new policy invented for
  this task. Kept in its own file, independent of
  NotificationModule/PrismaModule, specifically so this logic is
  directly unit-testable without pulling in the database layer.

New: logging-notification-delivery.observer.ts
- Replaces the no-op observer with real (not discarded) metadata-only
  structured logging: tenantId, deliveryId, channel, attemptCount,
  outcome, errorCode -- exactly the fields NotificationObservation
  already carries, nothing more.

Real defect found and fixed in existing, previously-unused code path:
@medsphere/config's loadEnv() always read the live process.env,
ignoring any caller-supplied environment, even though nothing in the
existing repository called it with an injected environment before
this task (confirmed zero other callers). This made the factory
function I needed to write untestable as designed. Fixed with a
backward-compatible optional second parameter defaulting to
process.env, preserving 100% of existing behavior for any future
single-argument caller.

notification.module.ts changes: removed the hardcoded
UnconfiguredNotificationProviderRegistry/NoopNotificationDeliveryObserver
wiring, delegating instead to the new factory and logging observer.
No other provider, controller, or export changed.

Dependency: added nodemailer (+ @types/nodemailer) to
apps/auth-service. Initially added nodemailer ^6.9.16, then found via
pnpm audit that this version carried 8 known vulnerabilities;
upgraded to ^8.0.11 (fixed 7 of them), then found via a second audit
pass that a HIGH-severity vulnerability (arbitrary file read /
full-response SSRF via the message-level raw option, GHSA-p6gq-j5cr-w38f)
